;; fib.async (owner L12): Clojure's future and future-call over the builtin spawn (stdlib §2.11, §4.15
;; rows future and future-call). Tranche 1 holds these two; promise, deliver, delay, the watches and
;; the rest of the module come later. It is `:require`d or `:use`d, never implicit (plan §4.1).
;; pmap, which §4.15 lists here too, is the prelude's (plan §6 L12 and L4): it stays there until the
;; library's Reducible can drive a pmap over any source, which no package of tranche 1 owns.
;;
;; (future body ..) is (spawn (fn () body ..)): a task whose value is the last form. The closure is the
;; one spawn takes, so it must be sendable and a captured Cell is refused with the checker's own words
;; (`cell cannot be shared between threads: closure capture c has type (Cell i64)`, at the future).
;; Where Clojure writes @f the task is waited for with @f or (join f), which are one: `@` on a Task is
;; join, the fourth built-in instance of Deref (types §2.9, the owner's rule of 2026-10-01; the checker's
;; solve_deref and the init of the built-in instances). Until that rule, @f was `no implementation of
;; Deref for (Task i64)`, a gap against the note of row `future` that case 658 pinned and now flips.
;;
;; The template names spawn as fib.prelude/spawn, the one spelling that skips the user's scope
;; (stdlib §6.3, plan §4.3 rule 5), so a program with a function of its own named spawn still
;; spawns (case 655). The macro is needed because the body is not a function the caller wrote
;; (stdlib §4.15: "a macro over spawn"); it costs one JIT module under fibc per program that uses
;; it (stdlib §6.3), which a program that does not `:use` fib.async does not pay.
(ns fib.async)

(defmacro future (... body)
  `(fib.prelude/spawn (fn () (do ~@body))))

(defun future-call (f: (fn :send () a)) -> (Task a)
  (fib.prelude/spawn f))

;; A task that trapped (stage 1 of exceptions, docs/design/exceptions.md 4.1, stdlib §2.10): the trap of a task ends that task and not
;; the process. `try-join` waits for the task as `join` does and returns what it came to: `(Ok v)`, or `(Err trap)` when a trap ended it.
;; `join` and `@t` of a task that trapped trap in the joiner with the task's own message (Clojure's `deref` of a failed future throws).
;; What the task's frames owned when it trapped is never freed (the audit calls it abandoned, `;; audit: abandoned`). The kind of the trap
;; (index, overflow, ...) comes with the catching form of stage 2; today a trap is its message. Out of memory, a thread that cannot be
;; started and a stack overflow are not traps a task survives: they end the process, whoever's they are.
(defstruct Trap (message: str))
(derive Eq Trap)

(defun try-join (t: (Task a)) -> (Result a Trap)
  (match (task-failure t)
    ((some message) (Err (Trap message)))
    (nil (Ok (join t)))))
